  "strategy": "Approach and rationale\nThe current approach relies on local optimization methods that may get stuck in suboptimal regions. My strategy is to use a multi-resolution optimization with Bayesian optimization to balance exploration and exploitation, combined with repair mechanisms to handle constraints. This addresses the issue of premature convergence by explicitly balancing exploration (testing new areas) and exploitation (refining promising areas). The algorithm starts with a coarse resolution, progressively refines it, and uses Bayesian optimization with Gaussian processes to guide the search. This allows focusing on promising regions while respecting the sum constraint.\n\nMathematical specification\n- Objective: Minimize C5 = max_k \u222b h(x)(1-h(x+k)) dx over k, with 0 \u2264 h(x) \u2264 1 and \u222b h(x) dx = 1.\n- Discretization: Grid size n (initially small, then iteratively increases), dx = 2/n.\n- Variables: Step function h defined on n grid points.\n- Reformulation: We use a piecewise constant representation with variable interpolation points.\n- Optimization: Bayesian optimization with Gaussian process surrogate (Mat\u00e9rn kernel) and Expected Improvement acquisition function.\n- Surrogate formulation: We approximate the expensive objective function using the Gaussian process, which is then minimized.\n- Repair: For infeasible solutions, we repair them by scaling the function to satisfy the sum constraint and clipping values to [0,1].\n- Tuning: Acquisition trade-off parameter optimized via early stopping.\n\nImplementation procedure\n1. Parent initialization:\n   - Validate input parent (n0=280) and repair it if sum \u2260 140.\n   - Define dimension multiplier and coarsest level for multi-resolution.\n\n2. Coarse level search:\n   - Initiate Bayesian optimization using Mat\u00e9rn kernel and Expected Improvement.\n   - Generate random candidates and repair them.\n   - Train Gaussian process surrogate per parameterization.\n   - Update acquisition function and sample new points until stopping criteria met.\n\n3. Refinement phase:\n   - Transfer best candidate to finer grids using interpolation scaling.\n   - Repair transferred candidates to maintain sum/clip.\n   - Monitor objective to ensure improvement.\n   - Update grid and re-optimize.\n\n4. Bayesian optimization:\n   - Handle constraints with repair function.\n   - Choose optimal candidate based on acquisition values.\n   - Update global best if significant improvement detected.\n\n5. Score computation:\n   - Implement exact integrator matching the evaluator.\n   - Use repair as fallback repair mechanism.\n   - Adjust parameters based on performance.\n\n6. Stopping criteria:\n   - Wall time: Check at each evaluation if within budget.\n   - No improvement threshold: If a minimum improvement isn't met, terminate.\n   - Constraint failure: Repair and re-add points.\n\nSearch choices and variations\n- Multi-resolution: Start coarse, scale up to final n.\n- Bayesian parameters: Acquisition trade-off, initial samples, expected improvement parameters.\n- Repair: Scaling adjustment, clipping, additive jitter maintenance.\n- Optimization hyperparameters: SLSQP iterations, repair proportion, grid steps.\n\nComputebudget and robustness\n- Time budget estimation: 1100s allocated for main optimization with multiple phases.\n- Wall clock enforcement: 100ms checks at expensive stages.\n- Gaussian process training: Balance by early termination upon excess time.\n- Repair fallback: Use previous repair in case of isNew minimal improvement iteration.\n\nValidation and return contract\n- Ensure h[i] \u2208 [0,1] and sum(h) = target via repair.\n- Objectively score with map_cheap to stay close to original.\n- Re-evaluate final candidate with map_exact.\n- Return best h, C5, and n.",
